  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <details class="faq-item" open><summary>Are dividends free money?</summary><div class="faq-answer">No. When a company pays a $1 dividend, the share price typically drops by about $1 on the ex-dividend date. The total value of your holding is roughly the same immediately before and after the ex-date; what changes is the form (cash vs. unrealized price). Over time, dividend-paying stocks can outperform non-payers because the cash is reinvested or spent.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>How much do I need to live off dividends?</summary><div class="faq-answer">At a 4% blended yield, you need roughly $1M for $40K of annual income. At 3%, closer to $1.3M. Use the <a href="/calculator/">Calculator</a> to run your own numbers with realistic yield, growth, and inflation assumptions. Most retirees target 4–6% blended yield with a mix of dividend growers and income satellites.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>What's the difference between qualified and non-qualified dividends?</summary><div class="faq-answer">Qualified dividends (mostly from US common stocks held more than 60 days around the ex-date) are taxed at long-term capital gains rates of 0%, 15%, or 20%. Non-qualified dividends (REITs, BDCs, MLPs, foreign stocks, ELN premiums, money market funds) are taxed at your marginal ordinary income rate, which can be much higher.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I reinvest or take the cash?</summary><div class="faq-answer">In a tax-advantaged account, always reinvest during accumulation. In a taxable account, reinvesting is still usually right right in if you don't need the cash, but remember you owe income tax on the distribution in the year it's paid even if it never hits your bank account.</div></details>
  <details class="faq-item"><summary>Is a Dividend Aristocrat a safe investment?</summary><div class="faq-answer">It's safer than a non-Aristocrat, but not safe. The Aristocrats list (25+ consecutive years of dividend increases) is a quality screen, not a guarantee. General Electric was an Aristocrat until 2009; AT&amp;T cut its dividend in 2022 after 36 years of increases. Always check payout ratio and cash flow coverage.</div></details>

